# Interface and implementation file colocating

When an interface and its implementing class are defined in the **same project**, put them in the **same file**. Name the file after the implementation (e.g. `OAuthTokenSource.cs` contains `IOAuthTokenSource` and `OAuthTokenSource`).

Do **not** create a separate `IThing.cs` alongside `Thing.cs` in that case.

```csharp
// ✅ GOOD — OAuthTokenSource.cs
public interface IOAuthTokenSource
{
    Task<OAuthTokenResponse> GetTokenAsync(...);
}

public sealed class OAuthTokenSource(...) : IOAuthTokenSource
{
    public Task<OAuthTokenResponse> GetTokenAsync(...) { ... }
}
```

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — split across IOAuthTokenSource.cs and OAuthTokenSource.cs in the same project
```

**Exception:** Keep the interface in its own project/file when it is a public contract in a Core (or similar) assembly and the implementation lives in a different project (e.g. `IFooConnector` in Foo.Core, `FooConnector` in Foo.Implementation).
